  "subtitle": "a three-position switch that ditches Morse timing for tone frequency — dits and dahs are equal duration, differentiated by pitch",
  "why_scout_cares": "The interaction model is genuinely bizarre: a frequency-encoded pseudo-Morse code where timing — the defining feature of Morse since 1844 — is thrown out entirely. The user moves a spring-centered three-position switch. Left produces high-frequency tones (dits), right produces low-frequency tones (dahs), and both are equal duration. Sound-on time equals sound-off time, making the code faster and less fatiguing. The system translates tone sequences to ASCII, implementing four keyboard modes (standard, shift, control, and macro sequence). Continuous dits/dahs beyond seven trigger cursor movement. Developed at Johns Hopkins Applied Physics Lab for the Veterans Administration. The user literally hears their own keystrokes as musical pitches.",
